#
# Crypto policy options — the decisions about *which* implementations a build
# may select and *what* an open is allowed to skip. They belong to this
# package, and this file is the single place they are defined: crypto's own
# Kconfig sources it into its "Crypto options" menu, and a tree that embeds
# crypto (un) sources the same file rather than keeping a copy in step by
# hand. Sourced as "$CRYPTO_DIR/Kconfig.crypto", which resolves in either tree.
#
# The file carries no menu of its own so each package can place these where its
# own menu structure wants them.
#
# CRYPTO_VERIFIED deliberately declares no default here. A bool without one is
# n, which is what crypto standalone wants, and leaving it absent is what lets
# an embedding package state its own policy — un declares "default y" and gets
# it. Adding a "default n" line here would not be a no-op: kconfig takes the
# first default in file order, so it would beat any override declared after the
# source line.
#

config CRYPTO_VERIFIED
	bool "Use only verified cryptography"
	help
	  When enabled, only formally verified or audited cryptographic
	  implementations are available for selection. Currently this
	  restricts algorithm choices to aws-lc provided implementations.

	  This is not a performance option, and it is not the trade the two
	  symbols below it are. Those buy speed by giving up an answer, and a
	  build that exists to measure throughput turns them off; this one it
	  leaves on, because there is nothing to give up. The aws-lc
	  implementations are the same hand-written assembly as the
	  alternatives, where the build targets the host CPU the default chains
	  pin their SHA-NI, AVX2 or ARMv8 variant and verified is as often the
	  faster route as the slower one. Saying n to go faster is backwards.

	  What n buys is a wider menu of implementations, and in this tree
	  that is a narrow thing: y hides the generic C and OpenSSL choices,
	  and the digests are the only algorithms that have both: SHA-1,
	  SHA-2 and SHA-3, with HMAC, PRF and HKDF following whichever digest
	  is selected. AES, ChaCha20-Poly1305, the groups and the transforms
	  have no OpenSSL variant to select and are aws-lc whatever this is
	  set to. So for the common suites (AES-GCM, ChaCha20-Poly1305,
	  X25519 and the NIST curves, SHA-2) the restriction is not felt at
	  all.

	  Where it is felt is a target aws-lc has no assembly for. Those
	  digests exist for x86_64 and armv8, so on any other architecture, or
	  under CONFIG_CC_CPU_ACCEL_NONE, y hides the portable C fallback and
	  leaves the digest choice nowhere to land: the SHA-1 and SHA-2
	  selectors come out "none" — no digest in the build at all — where n
	  would have selected the generic implementation. Say n there.

	  One caveat when measuring rather than shipping: OpenSSL modules
	  dispatch on CPUID/hwcaps at run time whatever the build targeted,
	  while the aws-lc variant is fixed at configure time. On a
	  portable-CPU build that holds aws-lc to baseline assembly while
	  OpenSSL still picks up the host's extensions, so a backend
	  comparison belongs on a build that targets the host CPU.

	  Needs the aws-lc submodule, which is initialized on demand.

config CRYPTO_VERIFIED_DECRYPT_AEAD
	bool "Verify the authentication tag when decrypting"
	default y
	help
	  Check that what came out of an AEAD open is what the sender put in,
	  and refuse the record when it is not.

	  Opening a record is two jobs and on a modern core they cost about
	  the same, so saying n — run the keystream, compute no authenticator
	  — is worth about 1.9x on AES-128-GCM here. ChaCha20-Poly1305 gains
	  almost nothing, because the fused assembly has no entry point that
	  skips the tag and declining to verify declines the fused backend
	  with it.

	  This is the AEAD half only. The CBC HMAC suites are not opened by an
	  AEAD backend at all — the record layer composes and checks that MAC
	  itself — and they have their own symbol,
	  CONFIG_CRYPTO_VERIFIED_DECRYPT_MAC.

	  What n costs is the guarantee: every decrypt reports success, so a
	  forgery, a rewritten header and a corrupted capture all become
	  plaintext indistinguishable from a good record, and the counter that
	  reported a failed tag stops counting. A decoder reading already
	  captured traffic is the case where that trade can be made at all —
	  but it is still the decoder's own answer about whether a record was
	  genuine that is being given up.

	  If unsure, say Y.

config CRYPTO_VERIFIED_DECRYPT_MAC
	bool "Verify the MAC when decrypting"
	default y
	help
	  The same decision as CONFIG_CRYPTO_VERIFIED_DECRYPT_AEAD, for the
	  ciphers that are not AEAD: the TLS 1.0-1.2 CBC suites, where the
	  authenticator is an HMAC that whoever composes the record checks
	  itself rather than something a cipher backend returns. This package
	  supplies the pieces; the symbol is read by the record layer that
	  puts them together (net/tls/hs in un). Say n and that layer decrypts
	  the record, strips the padding and the MAC, and delivers the
	  plaintext without computing the HMAC over it.

	  The HMAC is most of the work in a CBC open — SHA-1 or SHA-256 over
	  the whole record next to an AES-CBC pass over the same bytes — so n
	  measures about 3-4x on the AES-CBC HMAC suites, the largest single
	  saving of any of these knobs. It applies to both compositions:
	  MAC-then-encrypt, and encrypt-then-MAC (RFC 7366), where the MAC is
	  over the ciphertext and is otherwise checked before decrypting.

	  What n costs is the same guarantee the AEAD symbol gives up, with
	  one difference in degree. A CBC record still has to decrypt to
	  well-formed padding of a plausible length, so a random forgery is
	  usually still refused — but it is refused by a structural check, not
	  by an authenticator, and anything that survives that check is
	  delivered as genuine plaintext. Under encrypt-then-MAC the trailing
	  MAC bytes are only sliced off, never checked, and a rewritten record
	  header no longer changes the answer at all.

	  The two symbols are independent so a measurement can attribute the
	  cost to one composition or the other, but a build that turns off
	  only one is verifying half its traffic. Nothing terminating a live
	  connection should carry either off.

	  If unsure, say Y.
